2186561104 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}2 t+16 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}16 t^{3} + 2 t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[t^{2}+4 t^{4}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\left(1\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(1\\right)^{4}]-[\\left(0\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(0\\right)^{4}] \\\\  &=[1+4]-[0+0] \\\\  &=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561105 1080 "14" 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561105 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561105 1083 #uuid "27339d99-5bd9-5b84-ada5-cae5e808d7a6" 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561106 1080 "\\frac{312}{5}" 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561106 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561106 1083 #uuid "2b805d00-fa7e-59e2-bf53-fb6bd5ba499b" 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561107 1080 "-3" 13194139536044 17592186045443 true] [17592186561107 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536044 1759